From 82d1fd6e0fd4b5aa463f6c02eec699d7996e3713 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 02:40:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: SDR of 8 bits goes out at 8 bits over HDMI on
 NVIDIA and Intel

Where nothing asks for a depth, nvidia-drm leaves it to NVKMS, which takes 10 bits per component
(its max_output_color_depth) and moves to HDMI FRL as soon as TMDS does not carry the mode at that
depth, without trying a lower one (GetDfpHdmiProtocol()). So the GUI at 2160p60 went out over FRL
at 10 bits, and at 2160p24 to 30 over TMDS at 371.25 MHz with scrambling, for a picture of 8 bits:
Kodi draws the GUI in a buffer of 8 bits per component, and an SDR video of 8 bits too. i915 does
the same at 12 bits where TMDS carries them (445.5 MHz at 2160p24 to 30). Windows sends SDR at 8
bits there: 2160p60 over TMDS at 594 MHz.

Now "max bpc" is 8 for SDR of 8 bits on an HDMI link of nvidia-drm (with NV_OUTPUT_BPC, the Hades
patches; an HDMI connector, or a dual-mode DisplayPort one driven as HDMI) and of i915 and xe. On
the LG C5: the GUI at 2160p60 over TMDS at 594 MHz, at 2160p24 to 30 at 297 MHz without scrambling;
at 119.88 and 120 Hz still FRL, at 8 bits (TMDS carries 2160p120 at no depth). What keeps the
drivers' choice: HDR10, HLG and HDR10+ (or 8 bits where the user chose that, "HDR output depth"),
and SDR of more than 8 bits (a video of 10 bits, in a half-float or 10-bit buffer), so that no
code of it is lost. Native Dolby Vision sets 8 bits for its transport itself, as before, and puts
back what it found, which is now 8 for the GUI: its end no longer moves the link from 297 MHz to
371.25 MHz at 2160p24, nor from TMDS to FRL at 2160p60.

Not amdgpu: its display core dithers an 8-bit stream by default (DITHER_OPTION_SPATIAL8) and not a
12-bit one, so its GUI at 2160p24 goes out at 12 bits with its codes as they are; at 8 bits they
would be dithered. Nor DisplayPort sinks: SDR keeps the drivers' choice there.

A change of "max bpc" is a modeset (connectors_changed in the DRM core; colorBpcChanged, a modeset
of NVKMS that sets the head again), so it goes only in a request that sets the head again anyway:
the commit of a change of mode, Kodi's first commit on an output, the request that turns the HDR
signalling on or off (on nvidia-drm the HDR InfoFrame and the colorimetry are a modeset of NVKMS
on every GPU that has no flip-synchronized InfoFrames, Turing to Blackwell, and any GPU with a
DisplayPort connector; on i915 and amdgpu entering or leaving HDR is a full modeset), and the end
of native Dolby Vision, also where it goes with the next mode in one commit. A lower depth
that would come in another request waits for the next such one. A higher one goes with the request
that needs it: with the change of mode for a video of more than 8 bits that opens (VideoPlayer says
the depth of the stream with the mode it asks for), or, where such a video starts in the mode of
the GUI, with its first commit, which then sets the head again (the one case where the depth costs
a change of the link). Kodi's first commit takes the GUI to 8 bits where the console left more.

The decision is in LinkDepth.h (LinkDepthFor() and what each request carries), tested on the host
with a model of the commits of the start and the end of every kind of video at GUI rates of 60,
119.88 and 120 Hz, against the behaviour before. It takes the place of HDRDepth.h: the setting
"HDR output depth" works as it did. Without debug logging, kodi.log says what Kodi asked for and
why ("Output depth: SDR goes out at 8 bits on ..."), and once per output what it does with it;
the kernel says how the link goes (nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9044: "Hades: head ...").
